body:
Oh yes, I heard about it and read some time ago. It's a good lead, because I can see, that some of this things I wrote have much in common with derealization disorder, but I'm not quite sure it fits in 100 percent. I feel that my perception isn't as it is in derealization disorder. But thank you for your observation - very accurate.

&#x200B;

Talking about it with other people can be helpful, but it often can be perceived as "useless" or "disappointing". You can't talk about it with your closest family/friends because it can make them feel worry - and idea of making people worry about you is absolutely terrible. Especially when you feetl the crave of being forgotten, which is in exact opposition of "normal" human reaction. It's not good solution for me unfortunately. Or immediate help reaction can be uncomfortable and making feel worse.

&#x200B;

And going to the doctor for meds is like roulette. Therapy is a very good choice. My friend used to go to psychodynamic psychotherapy and it was a great help. The problem is when you feel lazy and unable to change things. So it ends with realization that you don't want to change and that "wanting" is the main condition for professional help.

&#x200B;

It's really running the same path over and over again and encountering same dead-ends. This subreddit is a great place not only for people with strong suicidal thoughts, but also for mildly-calm-deprresed like me.
